Mathematical & System Modeling Section

Q-HISS Scientific Publication Edition — v1.0

This section defines a minimal but comprehensive modeling backbone for Q-HISS: (1) spacecraft dynamics, (2) perception & state estimation, (3) layered decision/control, (4) energy/thermal/resource management, (5) fault management, and (6) governance constraints with auditable authority.

⸻

1. System State, Inputs, and Observations

Let the spacecraft be modeled as a partially observed dynamical system:
   •   State: x_t \in \mathbb{R}^{n} (orbit/attitude, subsystem health, resources, thermal states)
   •   Control: u_t \in \mathbb{R}^{m} (actuators, power routing, thermal valves, comm modes)
   •   Disturbances: w_t (solar pressure, radiation events, component drift)
   •   Observations: y_t \in \mathbb{R}^{p} (sensors, telemetry, star tracker, inertial, temps)

Dynamics:
x_{t+1} = f(x_t, u_t, w_t) + \epsilon_t
Observation model:
y_t = h(x_t) + \nu_t

Where \epsilon_t, \nu_t capture process/measurement noise.

⸻

2. Spacecraft Dynamics: Orbit + Attitude (Baseline)

2.1 Translational (orbit) dynamics (continuous-time)

\dot{r} = v,\quad \dot{v} = a_g(r) + a_{pert}(t) + a_{prop}(u_t)
   •   r, v \in \mathbb{R}^3
   •   a_g(r): central gravity (or n-body in deep space)
   •   a_{pert}: perturbations (SRP, third-body, drag in LEO, etc.)
   •   a_{prop}: propulsion acceleration command

2.2 Rotational (attitude) dynamics

Represent attitude by quaternion q and body rates \omega:
\dot{q} = \tfrac{1}{2}\Omega(\omega)q,\quad
J\dot{\omega} = \tau(u_t) - \omega \times (J\omega) + \tau_{dist}
   •   J: inertia matrix
   •   \tau(u_t): reaction wheels / thrusters torque
   •   \tau_{dist}: disturbance torques

⸻

3. State Estimation and Sensor Fusion

Q-HISS uses a multi-rate estimator across navigation + health monitoring.

3.1 Navigation filter (generic EKF / UKF form)

\hat{x}_{t|t-1} = f(\hat{x}_{t-1|t-1}, u_{t-1}, 0)
K_t = P_{t|t-1}H_t^\top(H_tP_{t|t-1}H_t^\top + R)^{-1}
\hat{x}_{t|t} = \hat{x}_{t|t-1} + K_t\big(y_t - h(\hat{x}_{t|t-1})\big)
   •   Supports star tracker + inertial + optical nav + celestial fixes

3.2 Health-state estimation (latent health z_t)

Introduce subsystem health latent variables z_t (battery SOH, reaction wheel friction, sensor bias):
z_{t+1} = g(z_t, x_t) + \eta_t
Estimated via Bayesian filtering / particle filters / HMMs depending on nonlinearity.

