4. Layered Autonomy as a Hierarchical Control System

Model Q-HISS as a two-timescale hierarchical controller:
   •   Reflex layer (fast): stability + safety reactions (ms–s)
   •   Cognitive layer (slow): planning + scheduling (minutes–hours)
   •   Governance layer (authoritative): constraint enforcement + authorization

4.1 Fast reflex controller

A stabilizing feedback law:
u_t^{ref} = \pi_{ref}( \hat{x}_t )
Typical forms:
   •   Attitude: LQR / MPC around reference q^\*
   •   Thermal: PID / constrained MPC for heaters/valves
   •   Power: safe-mode routing policy when anomalies detected

4.2 Cognitive planning as constrained optimization

Plan a sequence U = \{u_t\}_{t=0}^{T} minimizing mission cost:
\min_{U} \ \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T} \ell(x_t,u_t) + \ell_T(x_T)\right]
Subject to:
x_{t+1}=f(x_t,u_t,w_t),\quad u_t \in \mathcal{U},\quad x_t \in \mathcal{X}
This can be implemented as:
   •   Model Predictive Control (MPC) for resource/thermal scheduling
   •   Mixed-Integer optimization for mode switching and discrete decisions

4.3 Decision-making under uncertainty (POMDP)

For deep-space uncertainty:
\mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{X},\mathcal{U},\mathcal{Y},T,O,R,\gamma)
Belief update:
b_{t+1}(x) \propto O(y_{t+1}|x)\int T(x|x',u_t)b_t(x')dx'
Policy:
u_t = \pi(b_t)
Used for: science ops, comm windows, risk-aware route changes, contingency response.

